{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Judges and officers shall you appoint for yourself, etc., and they shall judge the people with just judgment.</b> It should have said 'and they shall judge you,' for the word 'you' would come in place of 'for yourself,' and in place of 'and they shall judge' it should have said 'and they shall judge' (in the imperfect), for 'וְשָׁפְטוּ' is not a commandment but merely a declaration that things will be so." "The explanation of the matter is that this verse is a commandment upon whoever has it in his power to appoint judges — that he must appoint them on the condition that they be judges who will not show favoritism even to the one who appoints them. And this is what it says: 'for yourself' — concerning yourself. And from this it follows by an a fortiori argument that they must judge all the people with just judgment, for if they will not show favoritism even to you, a fortiori to all the people. Therefore it says 'וְשָׁפְטוּ' in the language of declaration, to say that so it will certainly be by an a fortiori argument. Not like the practice of our generation, in which everyone who has it in his power to appoint judges selects his relative or his acquaintance — it is as though he stipulated with him explicitly on the condition that he show him favoritism, and so they do. And it follows from this that also all the people, even those who are distant, will not be judged with justice, for the judge must equalize all the cases, so that they should not say to him: yesterday you ruled thus and so for your relative or for your acquaintance or for a fellow townsman, and now you change the law in such a case." "And by way of allusion one may say that everywhere 'the people' is mentioned it speaks of the wicked, as Rashi explained on the verse 'and the people were as complainers' (Numbers 11:1). And our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot 1:8): when the litigants stand before you, let them be in your eyes as wicked, etc. Therefore it says 'and they shall judge the people,' for at the time of judgment they should be in your eyes like the rest of 'the people,' by whom the wicked are meant. But then 'just judgment' — after the judgment the wickedness turns to righteousness, for they should be in your eyes as righteous once they have accepted the judgment upon themselves. Another matter: 'Judges and officers shall you appoint for yourself' — straighten yourself first (Bava Metzia 107a), and afterward 'and they shall judge the people' — to straighten others, for any judge who removes money from it through judgment is not a judge (Bava Batra 58a)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:44.114617012Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}